WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.060
Partner without debt, use funds well, and plan your next round.

2
00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:09.300
This is the video for module 3 of Accessing Grants, Loans and Partnerships.

3
00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:20.230
A good partnership can do what a loan does, without interest or collateral. But selling to big buyers on credit creates a waiting period you must fund.

4
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:27.480
Getting money is half the job. How you use and report it decides whether you are ever funded again.

5
00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:41.140
Ada makes chin-chin in Port Harcourt. A supermarket chain offers to take 600 packs a month at 1,200 naira each, paid 30 days after the end of each delivery month.

6
00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:50.950
Each pack costs her about 750 naira, so she makes 450 naira a pack, or 270,000 naira a month.

7
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.400
But she must spend 450,000 naira a month to produce them.

8
00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:05.500
Because payment comes late, she funds about two months first: 900,000 naira. That waiting period is the cash gap.

9
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:22.370
She has only 300,000 saved. So her supplier gives 30-day credit, and she starts with two branches instead of three. Her cash need falls to about 350,000 naira, close to her savings. Partnerships, not a loan.

10
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:35.480
Write every partnership down. Name both sides, what each provides and when, prices and payment terms, who owns what, how long it lasts and how to end it, and signatures with dates.

11
00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:47.330
Never hand over your recipe or customer list without clear terms. And anyone asking for a share of your business needs proper legal advice and registration with CAC.

12
00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:53.290
Keep grant or loan money in a separate account, used only for that purpose.

13
00:01:54.540 --> 00:02:00.470
Pay suppliers by transfer, so every payment shows on the statement. Keep cash payments rare.

14
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:09.340
For every payment, keep the invoice, the transfer receipt, and a delivery note or photo. Photograph receipts the same day.

15
00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:22.580
Compare your plan with what you actually spent every week. And report problems early and plainly. A funder who hears about a delay from you reacts very differently from one who discovers it.

16
00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:32.850
Bukola runs a catering business in Ilorin. She receives a 1,000,000 naira grant for a gas cooker, a chest freezer and hygiene training.

17
00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:41.420
The cooker price rose between quote and purchase: 470,000 naira, 20,000 more than planned.

18
00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:48.940
But the freezer cost 15,000 less and delivery 5,000 less, with training as planned.

19
00:02:50.190 --> 00:03:06.790
The total is exactly 1,000,000 naira. Her agreement allows moves of up to 10% of a line, and 20,000 is about 4% of the cooker line, so she explains it in one line of her report. A bigger change would need written approval first.

20
00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:17.110
Funded growth often brings equipment and new hands. If equipment uses gas, electricity or heat, have a qualified person install it.

21
00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.130
Keep a fire extinguisher where staff can reach it.

22
00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:36.820
Train on safety first, and show every worker how to switch off the gas and power. Show, then watch, then check their work daily for the first two weeks. An accident hurts people first, and it also ends funding relationships.

23
00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:55.010
For example, Ruth makes juice and zobo in Jos. She needs equipment costing about 850,000 naira. She mixes 200,000 of her own savings, a hotel deposit of about 150,000, and a 500,000 naira loan.

24
00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:05.860
The payment is about 47,280 naira a month. Her worst recent month had 60,000 free, so the bad-month test passes.

25
00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:19.010
In 90 days she closes her last gaps, applies with her full Funding Readiness Pack, then buys, trains and starts deliveries. Her score has risen from 6 to 10 out of 12.

26
00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:32.910
To remember. Partnerships can replace a loan. Calculate the cash gap before saying yes to a big buyer. Write every deal down. Keep funded money separate, pay by transfer and keep receipts.

27
00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:40.450
Report results in numbers, and problems early. And saying no to an offer that does not fit is a good decision.

28
00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:51.430
Your tasks: design one partnership deal with its cash gap, build your funded-money tracker, and assemble your Funding Readiness Pack with a 90-day plan.

29
00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:53.720
Continue with the lessons below.
